ROCKFORD, MI -- Drinking water tested at East Rockford Middle School shows no signs of Wolverine World Wide tannery waste chemicals.
Two weeks ago, drinking fountains at the school were turned off as a precaution after leather and rubber scraps were found less than a mile northeast of the school on Ramsdell Drive. Other sites in the area also have been tested after scraps from Wolverine tannery operations years ago came to light.
Well test results show no presence of chemical contamination at the school or the property off Ramsdell, Wolverine announced on Wednesday, Oct. 25. Staff and the 800 students at the middle school at 8615 9 Mile Road have been drinking bottled water since Oct. 12.
The water will be turned back on at the school Wednesday morning, said Rockford Superintendent Michael Shibler in an 8 a.m. email to parents and staff.
"I have received the analytical report from Rose & Westra, the laboratory assigned to test the water quality at East Rockford Middle School," Shibler wrote. "I am happy to report that there were no PFAS detected in the water at ERMS. This is great news!"
Wolverine and the Michigan Department of Environmental Quality also confirmed the testing results.
"As we had expected, the test of water samples from East Rockford Middle School and the Ramsdell property confirm safe water," said Chris Hufnagel, senior vice president of strategy for Wolverine World Wide, in a statement.
"The waste at the property on Ramsdell Drive was primarily construction debris with a few leather and rubber scraps, and PFAS were not detected in the wells," he said. "Based on our review, we confirmed this waste was disposed of in the 1970s with the permission of the landowner at the time. We appreciate the current property owner bringing this to the MDEQ's attention."
Abigail Hendershott, acting remediation division supervisor in the DEQ Grand Rapids office, said before test results returned on Tuesday it was still unclear whether liquid waste was dumped off Ramsdell or if that location, an old gravel pit, was only used to dispose of leather scraps and debris.
The school testing was sparked by reported evidence of historical waste disposal on land off Ramsdell between 9 Mile and 10 Mile Roads. The DEQ inspected the property and found leather scraps. The property owner had documents indicating a previous owner had allowed Wolverine to use the gravel pit as a disposal area.
Recent experience in Kent County has demonstrated that these conditions may indicate the presence of PFAS in groundwater, the DEQ said.
"We are happy to see that the results demonstrate the school has not been affected by this potential contamination," Hendershott said in a statement Wednesday. "The DEQ's primary focus has been to ensure the safety of the students, staff and faculty which is why we advised precautionary efforts."
Wolverine toxic water testing area spreads across Belmont
This year, toxic chemicals from industrial waste the shoe company dumped during the 1960s was detected in private residential drinking water wells. Much of the focus has been on tannery sludge dumped at a 76-acre landfill at 1855 House St. NE in Plainfield Township, although other dump sites continue to emerge.
