There's no way around it, Sam Cooke was one cool mother. Not only did he help to pioneer soul music, not only did he rack up over thirty Top 40 hits throughout his career, and not only was he a leader throughout the Civil Rights Movement, but he did it all as one of the mid-century's best dressed guys.
Take this photo of Cooke from 1960, for example. We could wax poetic on just about every detail of his outfit, but what stands out the most are those tattersall trousers. Sure, there's a helluva lot going on with them, but Cooke clearly knew a thing or two about sartorial balance, letting the statement piece do all the talking, and keeping everything else nice and quiet.
That's why, while his patterned pants (with some pretty amazing jean-like pockets) are as bold as can be, they hardly look out of place. His navy blazer, stark white shirt, and dark tie all serve as able complements, but none are fighting for the spotlight. Oh, and even though it doesn't really shift the balance of the outfit, we'd be remiss if we didn't note Cooke's thoughtful, yet tragically underutilized, finishing touch: the tie tuck.
So next time you pick up a pair of pants and think, "These might just be too much," remember: It's all about the balance.
Jake Gallagher is a writer living in Manhattan. He writes the blog Wax Wane.
